The code statements that lead to the bug are:
struct sock *sk, *parent = chan->data;
lock_sock(parent);
Reason:
The variable `parent` is assigned from `chan->data`, which can be NULL if the parent socket was already released. Calling `lock_sock(parent)` on a NULL pointer causes a null-ptr-deref.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!parent)
    return NULL;
Reason:
This adds a NULL check before using `parent`, returning NULL immediately if it is NULL, preventing the null-ptr-deref. This matches the guards already present in similar callback functions.

